<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <!--
      Everything that talks to the hotel's chain node: the JSON-RPC calls, and the two contracts
      those calls reach. Nothing in here is cryptography — the signing, the key derivation, the RLP
      and the ABI all live in Vortex.Crypto/Ethereum and are pure functions with no node behind
      them. What is here is the part that needs a URL, a timeout and a socket, and that is the whole
      reason it is a separate assembly rather than another folder there: Vortex.Crypto has no
      HttpClient and should not acquire one.

      Callers resolve IChainService from Vortex.Primitives and never reference this project, the same
      way Vortex.WebApi reaches the shop. So a hotel that runs no chain simply has a service that
      answers "off", and nothing above it has to know.
    -->
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Vortex.Chain.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      No Microsoft.Extensions.Http, and therefore no IHttpClientFactory. The factory exists to stop
      code that news up an HttpClient per request from exhausting sockets; this talks to exactly one
      node at one URL and holds one client for the life of the process, which is the shape the
      factory is trying to produce. Adding it would mean a new entry in Directory.Packages.props for
      a problem this does not have.

      The one thing given up is the factory's handler rotation, which is what lets a long-lived
      client notice a DNS change. The node is a fixed compose hostname; if that ever stops being
      true, this is the paragraph that says what to reach for.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Vortex.Crypto\Vortex.Crypto.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Vortex.Primitives\Vortex.Primitives.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
